Day 6: Type of ML — instance based vs model based

In machine learning, the simple way of learning anything is remembering everything, like memorizing.

Or generalizing. We learn the concept.
Example: in chemistry we often try to memorize. In physics we learn concepts.

In the future, while we are learning ML algorithms, we should know if it is model based learning or instance based learning.

Instance based:

IQ | CGPA | placement
80 | 8 | y
70 | 7 | n

Then for 7.5 | 10.3 — will it be yes?

Since we have not implemented any model, we will look for similarity. Similarity is measured by distance.

We will take the nearest points, like 3 nearest.
If most nearest points say yes, then we get placement yes.

This is instance based learning. This is called KNN.

For instance based learning we do not train the model. We react according to the data.

Now model based learning:

Same data:

IQ | CGPA | placement
80 | 8 | y
70 | 7 | n

For 7.5 | 10.3 — will it be yes?

In model based learning the model will understand the behavior of the data.
It will make a boundary on the graph.
If the point is in the X region it will be fail, if in the O region then pass.

In model based learning they find the mathematical input and output and we get the decision function. We can classify the incoming data point.

Suppose all our data is removed but we still have the decision function. We do not need the old data now. When a new point comes we can find the result.

Examples: linear regression, decision tree. There are many model based algorithms.
Instance based has very few algorithms.

Model based and instance based both need prepared data.

In model based:
We generate a model. It is just a set of parameters. Example in linear regression the parameters are slope and intercept.

In instance based learning we do not train a model.

In model based we store the model in a suitable form.

In model based there is a rule inside the model.

In instance based: no generation before scoring.

In model based: no need of training data once trained for scoring.
But in instance based, training data is required at scoring time.

In model based: we do not need very high storage. We just store the equation.
In instance based the storage is heavy.

Instance based is also known as lazy learners.
